{"name":"Lilliputian Lime","hex":"#88dd55","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lilliputian","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very small person or being.","A fruit fly gene that, when mutated, makes cells abnormally small. See AFF2."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Very small, diminutive","Trivial, petty"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lilliputian"},{"term":"lime","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any inorganic material containing calcium, usually calcium oxide (quicklime) or calcium hydroxide (slaked lime).","Any gluey or adhesive substance that traps or captures; sometimes a synonym for birdlime."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To treat with calcium hydroxide or calcium oxide (lime).","To smear with birdlime.\n To ensnare, catch, entrap."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lime"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lilliput and Blefuscu","blocks":[{"text":"Lilliput and Blefuscu are two fictional island nations that appear in the first part of the 1726 satirical prose novel Gulliver's Travels by Jonathan Swift. The two islands are neighbours in the South Indian Ocean, separated by a channel 800 yards (730 m) wide. Both are inhabited by tiny people who are about one-twelfth the height of ordinary human beings. Both nations are empires and the capital of Lilliput is Mildendo."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Moll_-_A_map_of_the_world_shewing_the_course_of_Mr_Dampiers_voyage_round_it_from_1679_to_1691_%28cropped%29_-_New_Holland_-_Dimens_Land.png/330px-Moll_-_A_map_of_the_world_shewing_the_course_of_Mr_Dampiers_voyage_round_it_from_1679_to_1691_%28cropped%29_-_New_Holland_-_Dimens_Land.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lilliput_and_Blefuscu","wikidata":"Q265955"},{"title":"Lime (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Lime is a color that is a shade of yellow-green, so named because it is a representation of the color of the citrus fruit lime. It is the color that is in between the web color chartreuse and yellow on the color wheel. Alternate names for this color included yellow-green, lemon-lime, lime green, or bitter lime."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/AosaHGkyuai_%28cropped%29_3.jpg/330px-AosaHGkyuai_%28cropped%29_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lime_(color)","wikidata":"Q906936"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Gulliver%27s_Travel.jpg/330px-Gulliver%27s_Travel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Gulliver%27s_Travel.jpg/330px-Gulliver%27s_Travel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gulliver inspecting the army of Lilliput","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGulliver's_Travel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Willmann%2C_Colin%2C_%26_Outhwaite%2C_Gulliver_and_the_Emperor_of_Lilliput%2C_cph.3b18907.jpg/500px-Willmann%2C_Colin%2C_%26_Outhwaite%2C_Gulliver_and_the_Emperor_of_Lilliput%2C_cph.3b18907.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Willmann%2C_Colin%2C_%26_Outhwaite%2C_Gulliver_and_the_Emperor_of_Lilliput%2C_cph.3b18907.jpg/500px-Willmann%2C_Colin%2C_%26_Outhwaite%2C_Gulliver_and_the_Emperor_of_Lilliput%2C_cph.3b18907.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gulliver and the Emperor of Lilliput, from a French edition of Gulliver's Travels (1850s)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWillmann%2C_Colin%2C_%26_Outhwaite%2C_Gulliver_and_the_Emperor_of_Lilliput%2C_cph.3b18907.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/39/Lilli